1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,125
John Turnbull Thomson: The
Surveyor Who Drew 1840s Singapore

2
00:00:05,125 --> 00:00:08,429
Thomson Road, Upper
Thomson, Thomson Plaza and

3
00:00:08,509 --> 00:00:11,813
the Thomson–East Coast
Line all carry the name

4
00:00:11,893 --> 00:00:15,350
of a man few Singaporeans
could say much about.

5
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:20,637
John Turnbull Thomson arrived in 1841
as the settlement's Government Surveyor,

6
00:00:20,717 --> 00:00:22,632
aged twenty, and for the next

7
00:00:22,712 --> 00:00:26,348
twelve years he measured,
mapped and built much of the

8
00:00:26,428 --> 00:00:30,350
settlement while also sketching
and painting what he saw.

9
00:00:30,350 --> 00:00:34,270
His pictures of the market on
the shore, the public buildings

10
00:00:34,350 --> 00:00:38,073
and the people at work are
some of the few images of 1840s

11
00:00:38,153 --> 00:00:42,336
Singapore made by someone who
lived there, and twenty years later

12
00:00:42,416 --> 00:00:46,350
he wrote a book about the place
and the people he had known.

13
00:00:46,350 --> 00:00:50,025
Thomson was born on the
10th of August 1821 at

14
00:00:50,105 --> 00:00:54,350
Glororum in Northumberland,
in the north of England.

15
00:00:54,350 --> 00:00:58,702
He studied mathematics at
Aberdeen and served an engineering

16
00:00:58,782 --> 00:01:03,799
apprenticeship in Newcastle upon
Tyne, and in 1838, still a teenager,

17
00:01:03,879 --> 00:01:09,050
he went out to the Straits of Malacca
to work as a surveyor in Penang.

18
00:01:09,050 --> 00:01:12,676
He spent about three years
there, mostly in the countryside,

19
00:01:12,756 --> 00:01:16,011
and later wrote that he had
come to know the villagers

20
00:01:16,091 --> 00:01:19,550
well enough to share their
small complaints and worries.

21
00:01:19,550 --> 00:01:24,537
On the 25th of October 1841 the
East India Company appointed him

22
00:01:24,617 --> 00:01:30,000
Government Surveyor for the Straits
Settlements, based in Singapore.

23
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,982
In 1844 he also took over
as Superintendent of Public

24
00:01:34,062 --> 00:01:38,427
Works from George Drumgoole
Coleman, the architect who had

25
00:01:38,507 --> 00:01:42,875
laid out much of the early
town and was leaving for home.

26
00:01:42,875 --> 00:01:46,465
For the next nine years one
young man was responsible

27
00:01:46,545 --> 00:01:50,344
for measuring the settlement's
land, charting its waters

28
00:01:50,424 --> 00:01:54,025
and designing and building
most of its public works.

29
00:01:54,025 --> 00:01:59,352
Thomson described Singapore on his
arrival as a town of 30,000 people,

30
00:01:59,432 --> 00:02:02,365
with "nearly every nation of the earth"

31
00:02:02,445 --> 00:02:06,925
represented but the Chinese
and Malays the largest groups.

32
00:02:06,925 --> 00:02:09,707
The European quarter was a district of

33
00:02:09,787 --> 00:02:13,100
handsome houses for
merchants and officials.

34
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:17,394
The Chinese town was more closely
built and busy with trade,

35
00:02:17,474 --> 00:02:20,456
and the Malays lived in villages of wooden

36
00:02:20,536 --> 00:02:24,400
houses with leaf-thatched
roofs on the edges of town.

37
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:27,888
Fires broke out often in
the closely packed quarters

38
00:02:27,968 --> 00:02:31,125
and could leave hundreds
of families homeless.

39
00:02:31,125 --> 00:02:32,703
The harbour, he wrote,

40
00:02:32,783 --> 00:02:36,625
was crowded with European,
Chinese and Malay ships.

41
00:02:36,625 --> 00:02:40,900
Only about two hundred
Europeans lived on shore.

42
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:45,902
In the evenings they met at Scandal
Point, on the shore near the Esplanade,

43
00:02:45,982 --> 00:02:48,341
at six o'clock to exchange the news,

44
00:02:48,421 --> 00:02:53,300
while married couples drove past in
their carriages and stopped to talk.

45
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:57,457
There were no banks yet, so each
firm kept its own iron safe,

46
00:02:57,537 --> 00:03:02,400
and Thomson remembered a merchant's
word as being as good as his bond.

47
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:06,225
He also recorded a
harder side of the town.

48
00:03:06,225 --> 00:03:10,286
Gang robberies happened almost
nightly in the poorer districts,

49
00:03:10,366 --> 00:03:14,954
and he blamed much of the crime on
secret societies and on a government

50
00:03:15,034 --> 00:03:19,175
that paid its senior officials
well but its police very little.

51
00:03:19,175 --> 00:03:23,623
He wrote that in his first four
years he came across the bodies of

52
00:03:23,703 --> 00:03:28,300
twenty murdered men on the public
roads within a few miles of town.

53
00:03:28,300 --> 00:03:33,410
The Company's government put a
full-time officer in charge of the

54
00:03:33,490 --> 00:03:38,600
police only in 1843, and Thomson
described this as long overdue.

55
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:41,875
Thomson's main job was measurement.

56
00:03:41,875 --> 00:03:44,883
He carried out the allotment
survey of the town,

57
00:03:44,963 --> 00:03:47,713
which fixed the boundaries
of plots of land,

58
00:03:47,793 --> 00:03:51,975
and a topographical survey of the
island and its smaller islands.

59
00:03:51,975 --> 00:03:56,328
In 1846 he published the
result as Plan of Singapore

60
00:03:56,408 --> 00:04:00,500
Town and Adjoining Districts
from Actual Survey.

61
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:04,038
The map shows the town along
the river, Kampong Glam and

62
00:04:04,118 --> 00:04:07,010
the roads running out
through the plantations,

63
00:04:07,090 --> 00:04:11,225
with property boundaries and the
names of the country districts.

64
00:04:11,225 --> 00:04:15,750
A copy is held by the
National Library of Singapore.

65
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:18,075
He also worked at sea.

66
00:04:18,075 --> 00:04:23,277
Between 1846 and the early 1850s
he made a marine survey of the

67
00:04:23,357 --> 00:04:28,975
Singapore Strait and of the east
coast of Johor up to Pulau Tioman.

68
00:04:28,975 --> 00:04:33,274
The British Admiralty published
his work as navigation charts

69
00:04:33,354 --> 00:04:37,375
and kept issuing corrected
editions of them for decades.

70
00:04:37,375 --> 00:04:41,125
The charts still carry
his name in their titles.

71
00:04:41,125 --> 00:04:44,236
Thomson had no formal
training as an artist,

72
00:04:44,316 --> 00:04:48,450
but he drew and painted
throughout his time in Singapore.

73
00:04:48,450 --> 00:04:52,898
Many of them were later collected
and published in books about him,

74
00:04:52,978 --> 00:04:57,100
and they show buildings and
scenes that have long since gone.

75
00:04:57,100 --> 00:05:00,093
His drawing of the
Public Offices shows the

76
00:05:00,173 --> 00:05:04,175
government's own building as
an orderly classical block.

77
00:05:04,175 --> 00:05:07,660
The view from Pearl's Hill
shows the town from above,

78
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:10,283
with the low roofs of the Chinese town,

79
00:05:10,363 --> 00:05:13,525
the river and its bridges
and the hills beyond.

80
00:05:13,525 --> 00:05:17,256
His drawing of Telok Ayer
Market shows the octagonal

81
00:05:17,336 --> 00:05:21,214
market hall standing on
stilts over the sea before the

82
00:05:21,294 --> 00:05:25,325
land in front of it was
reclaimed later in the century.

83
00:05:25,325 --> 00:05:28,075
He also drew people at work.

84
00:05:28,075 --> 00:05:31,544
One page of his sketches
shows Hakka stonecutters

85
00:05:31,624 --> 00:05:33,644
on Pulau Ubin at their trade,

86
00:05:33,724 --> 00:05:37,925
squatting over blocks of granite
with hammers and chisels.

87
00:05:37,925 --> 00:05:41,413
Stonecutters like these
quarried and dressed the

88
00:05:41,493 --> 00:05:46,169
granite for his largest project,
the lighthouse on Pedra Branca,

89
00:05:46,249 --> 00:05:48,925
which was first lit in October 1851.

90
00:05:48,925 --> 00:05:53,775
That story is told in an earlier
post on Horsburgh Lighthouse.

91
00:05:53,775 --> 00:05:57,894
Thomson's interest in the
interior took him up the Kallang

92
00:05:57,974 --> 00:06:02,100
River by canoe with a few
friends and five Malay boatmen.

93
00:06:02,100 --> 00:06:06,007
He described tall trees shading
the stream, snakes on the

94
00:06:06,087 --> 00:06:10,203
branches overhead and Chinese
gambier and pepper plantations

95
00:06:10,283 --> 00:06:14,200
in the jungle, where the
planters gave the visitors tea.

96
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:18,725
Near a great swamp the ground
was covered in tiger footprints.

97
00:06:18,725 --> 00:06:22,881
Tigers were a real danger to
plantation workers at the time,

98
00:06:22,961 --> 00:06:26,341
and he described how a
tiger caught in a pit trap

99
00:06:26,421 --> 00:06:30,375
became an occasion for the
European community to gather.

100
00:06:30,375 --> 00:06:32,968
The government paid a reward of 100

101
00:06:33,048 --> 00:06:35,950
Spanish dollars for each tiger's head.

102
00:06:35,950 --> 00:06:41,125
An earlier post covers the years
when Singapore had wild tigers.

103
00:06:41,125 --> 00:06:44,509
One of the friends Thomson
wrote about most warmly

104
00:06:44,589 --> 00:06:47,775
was Hoo Ah Kay, the
merchant known as Whampoa.

105
00:06:47,775 --> 00:06:52,212
Thomson first knew him as a boy
helping his father in a shop in

106
00:06:52,292 --> 00:06:57,025
the Chinese town, supplying ships
with beef, bread and vegetables.

107
00:06:57,025 --> 00:07:01,808
Later Thomson was a regular guest
at Whampoa's orange garden at Toa

108
00:07:01,888 --> 00:07:06,750
Payoh and at his dinners, where
naval officers and merchants mixed.

109
00:07:06,750 --> 00:07:09,951
Whampoa once looked around
Thomson's dining room,

110
00:07:10,031 --> 00:07:12,228
which was hung with oil paintings,

111
00:07:12,308 --> 00:07:16,125
and said he wished he could
"scratch" as well as Thomson.

112
00:07:16,125 --> 00:07:20,684
Thomson teased him about the difference
between "scratch" and "sketch",

113
00:07:20,764 --> 00:07:23,885
but when Whampoa asked
him to paint a portrait of

114
00:07:23,965 --> 00:07:27,950
his father before the old man
left for China, Thomson did so.

115
00:07:27,950 --> 00:07:31,800
He later saw it kept with
care in Whampoa's house.

116
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:35,232
Whampoa's own story is
told in an earlier post on

117
00:07:35,312 --> 00:07:38,825
the merchant who brought
Singapore its first ice.

118
00:07:38,825 --> 00:07:42,142
Thomson also admired
Abdullah bin Abdul Kadir,

119
00:07:42,222 --> 00:07:46,129
the Malay teacher and writer
known as Munshi Abdullah,

120
00:07:46,209 --> 00:07:49,975
and quoted his account of
the early town at length.

121
00:07:49,975 --> 00:07:56,283
In 1874 Thomson published an English
translation of parts of the Hikayat Abdullah,

122
00:07:56,363 --> 00:08:00,025
which helped bring the
book to English readers.

123
00:08:00,025 --> 00:08:04,125
An earlier post covers
Munshi Abdullah's life.

124
00:08:04,125 --> 00:08:08,684
As Superintendent of Public
Works, Thomson designed or built

125
00:08:08,764 --> 00:08:13,325
a wide range of the settlement's
public works in the 1840s.

126
00:08:13,325 --> 00:08:16,922
The best known is the
hospital for poor Chinese

127
00:08:17,002 --> 00:08:20,991
patients on Pearl's Hill,
paid for by Tan Tock Seng,

128
00:08:21,071 --> 00:08:24,200
whose foundation stone was laid in 1844.

129
00:08:24,200 --> 00:08:29,157
He built the first bridge across the
Kallang River, known as Thomson Bridge,

130
00:08:29,237 --> 00:08:32,750
and repaired Coleman Bridge
over the Singapore River.

131
00:08:32,750 --> 00:08:36,502
He added a spire to Saint
Andrew's Church, and he is

132
00:08:36,582 --> 00:08:40,261
credited with the Dalhousie
Obelisk, put up in 1850

133
00:08:40,341 --> 00:08:44,100
to mark the visit of the
Governor-General of India.

134
00:08:44,100 --> 00:08:48,626
He also laid out roads running
from the town into the interior,

135
00:08:48,706 --> 00:08:51,850
including the road that
now bears his name.

136
00:08:51,850 --> 00:08:55,915
Much of this work was done by
convict labourers from India,

137
00:08:55,995 --> 00:09:00,350
as was common practice in the
Straits Settlements at the time.

138
00:09:00,350 --> 00:09:05,345
In 1852 he also wrote a report
on how the town could be supplied

139
00:09:05,425 --> 00:09:10,025
with clean water, an issue
that later governments took up.

140
00:09:10,025 --> 00:09:13,179
Thomson's health broke down after years of

141
00:09:13,259 --> 00:09:16,799
fieldwork in the tropics,
and in August 1853 he

142
00:09:16,879 --> 00:09:20,575
left Singapore on sick
leave and did not return.

143
00:09:20,575 --> 00:09:23,740
That month, the Singapore
Free Press reported,

144
00:09:23,820 --> 00:09:26,984
leading residents held
a well-attended meeting

145
00:09:27,064 --> 00:09:30,450
in his honour at the News
Room in Raffles Place.

146
00:09:30,450 --> 00:09:35,014
He spent two years in Britain
and travelled in Europe,

147
00:09:35,094 --> 00:09:39,050
and early in 1856 he
emigrated to New Zealand.

148
00:09:39,050 --> 00:09:43,315
In New Zealand he became Chief
Surveyor of the Otago Province

149
00:09:43,395 --> 00:09:48,025
and explored and mapped much of
the interior of the South Island.

150
00:09:48,025 --> 00:09:50,695
He laid out the town of Invercargill and

151
00:09:50,775 --> 00:09:53,800
went on painting the
landscapes he surveyed.

152
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:58,205
On the 1st of May 1876 he
was appointed the first head

153
00:09:58,285 --> 00:10:02,193
of the country's new
national Survey Department,

154
00:10:02,273 --> 00:10:06,675
as Surveyor-General, and he
held the post until 1879.

155
00:10:06,675 --> 00:10:12,876
He wrote Some Glimpses into Life in the
Far East over the long winter evenings,

156
00:10:12,956 --> 00:10:16,772
about twenty years after
the events it describes;

157
00:10:16,852 --> 00:10:22,100
it was published in London in 1864,
and a sequel followed in 1865.

158
00:10:22,100 --> 00:10:25,593
He died at his home in Invercargill

159
00:10:25,673 --> 00:10:29,450
on the 16th of October 1884, aged 63.

160
00:10:29,450 --> 00:10:33,079
A statue of him with a
surveyor's theodolite stands

161
00:10:33,159 --> 00:10:36,352
at Ranfurly in Central
Otago, and a mountain,

162
00:10:36,432 --> 00:10:40,650
a waterfall and a park in New
Zealand are named after him.

163
00:10:40,650 --> 00:10:44,900
In Singapore his name
survives mainly on the map.

164
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:49,065
Thomson Road follows the line
of one of the roads he laid out

165
00:10:49,145 --> 00:10:53,240
into the interior, and Upper
Thomson Road, Old Upper Thomson

166
00:10:53,320 --> 00:10:57,425
Road and the surrounding Thomson
district all come from it.

167
00:10:57,425 --> 00:11:00,382
The name passed on to Thomson Plaza,

168
00:11:00,462 --> 00:11:06,200
Thomson Medical Centre and, in 2020,
to the Thomson–East Coast Line.

169
00:11:06,200 --> 00:11:09,818
His charts, his 1846
plan and his drawings are

170
00:11:09,898 --> 00:11:13,758
held by the National
Library, the National Museum

171
00:11:13,838 --> 00:11:17,375
of Singapore and
collections in New Zealand.

172
00:11:17,375 --> 00:11:21,407
Why it matters today: Much of
what we can picture of 1840s

173
00:11:21,487 --> 00:11:25,874
Singapore comes from one man,
who was paid to measure the place

174
00:11:25,954 --> 00:11:30,350
but also chose to draw it and
later to write about its people.

175
00:11:30,350 --> 00:11:34,767
His surveys fixed the boundaries
of land and roads that still shape

176
00:11:34,847 --> 00:11:38,929
parts of the city, and his
pictures and memoir record a small,

177
00:11:39,009 --> 00:11:43,775
rough and crowded port town in the
years before photography reached it.

178
00:11:43,775 --> 00:11:48,346
His name is on a road, a district
and an MRT line that thousands

179
00:11:48,426 --> 00:11:53,150
of people use every day, while
his story is much less well known.
